    <!-- ═══ 3. THE STORY OF THIS HOUSE ═══ -->
    <section class="bh-story" id="story" data-theme="light" aria-labelledby="storyTitle">
      <div class="ve-wrap bh-story__grid">
        <div class="bh-story__stick">
          <p class="ve-label ve-kicker">History</p>
          <h2 class="ve-h2" id="storyTitle" data-split>The story of <em>this house.</em></h2>
          <p class="bh-story__year" aria-hidden="true"><span>1867</span></p>
        </div>
        <ol class="bh-story__list">
          <li data-year="1867"><p class="bh-story__when">1867</p><h3>James Taylor plants the first Ceylon tea</h3><p>Five kilometres from here, the Scottish planter James Taylor plants 21 acres of tea on Loolecondera Estate, Field No. 7: the first commercial tea plantation in Ceylon. An industry is born.</p></li>
          <li data-year="c.1890"><p class="bh-story__when">Circa 1890</p><h3>The bungalow is built</h3><p>Galaha Estate builds this planter&rsquo;s residence for its British superintendent, in the decade Ceylon tea exports pass 20,000 tons a year. The house is a product of tea country&rsquo;s golden age.</p></li>
          <li data-year="1892"><p class="bh-story__when">1892</p><h3>James Taylor dies at Loolecondera</h3><p>Taylor spent forty years at Loolecondera. He died aged 57 and is buried at Mahaiyawa Cemetery in Kandy; his memorial at Loolecondera is passed by every Pekoe Trail Stage 2 walker.</p></li>
          <li data-year="1976"><p class="bh-story__when">1976</p><h3>Nationalisation: the JEDB era</h3><p>Land reform brings Galaha Estate under the Janatha Estates Development Board, and the bungalow becomes part of the state-managed plantation system. The tea keeps growing; the house keeps standing.</p></li>
          <li data-year="1990s"><p class="bh-story__when">The 1990s</p><h3>Back to private management</h3><p>Sri Lanka&rsquo;s tea estates return, step by step, to private management, and the estate and its bungalow begin a new chapter.</p></li>
          <li data-year="2026"><p class="bh-story__when">2026</p><h3>The Tea Bungalow opens</h3><p>The bungalow is restored as an intimate heritage stay, part of the Old Ceylon Heritage Collection. The fire is lit, the Long Table is set, and the first guests arrive. The story continues.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

